  "method": "Built by script from the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics' published tables, with no rewriting of the figures. Nonfatal injuries and illnesses come from the Survey of Occupational Injuries and Illnesses (SOII), an establishment survey of private industry employers based on their OSHA injury and illness records; about 200,000 employers report each year. This page uses its private industry estimates for 2024, released 2026-01-22. SOII rates are cases per 100 full-time equivalent workers, calculated by BLS as (cases ÷ hours worked) × 200,000; rates by event are per 10,000 full-time equivalent workers. Counts exclude farms with fewer than 11 employees. Fatal injuries come from the Census of Fatal Occupational Injuries (CFOI), a count of all fatal work injuries in the U.S. in the calendar year, built from over 25,500 source documents for 2024, covering all workers, including the self-employed and government workers; it excludes deaths from occupational illnesses. This page uses CFOI data for 2024, released 2026-02-19. Fatal injury rates are per 100,000 full-time equivalent workers, calculated by BLS as (deaths ÷ hours worked) × 200,000,000. Tables were downloaded on 2026-11-12. BLS publishes new SOII data each autumn and new CFOI data later; this page is rebuilt for each new data year.",
